ewcommand{\Gaunt}{{\mathcal G}} Complex and Real Spherical Harmonics ¶ Complex Spherical Harmonics ¶ Complex spherical harmonics, \Ylm \Ylm , are defined as the eigenfunctions of the orbital angular momentum operators, \hat L^2 \hat L^2 and \hat L_z \hat L_z . They can be labeled by two quantum numbers, l l and m m , related to the eigenvalues of \hat L^2 \hat L^2 and \hat L_z \hat L_z :  egin{equation} \hat L^2 \Ylm(	heta,\phi) = l(l+1) \Ylm(	heta,\phi), \end{equation}  egin{equation} \hat L^2 \Ylm(	heta,\phi) = l(l+1) \Ylm(	heta,\phi), \end{equation} and  egin{equation} \hat L_z \Ylm(	heta,\phi) = m \Ylm(	heta,\phi). \end{equation}  egin{equation} \hat L_z \Ylm(	heta,\phi) = m \Ylm(	heta,\phi). \end{equation} Their explicit expression is:  egin{equation} \Ylm(	heta,\phi) = (-1)^m N_\lm P_m^l(\cos	heta) e^{im\phi}, \end{equation}  egin{equation} \Ylm(	heta,\phi) = (-1)^m N_\lm P_m^l(\cos	heta) e^{im\phi}, \end{equation} where l l takes non-negative integer values and the possible values for m m are integers from -l -l to l l . The two symbols 	heta 	heta and \phi \phi are used to denote angular spherical coordinates. N_\lm N_\lm is the normalization factor:  egin{equation} N_\lm = \sqrt{ rac{2l+1}{4\pi} \dfrac{(l-m)!}{(l+m)!}} \end{equation}  egin{equation} N_\lm = \sqrt{ rac{2l+1}{4\pi} \dfrac{(l-m)!}{(l+m)!}} \end{equation} with P_m^l P_m^l being the associated Legendre polynomials. Complex spherical harmonics are orthogonal functions on the sphere, i.e.:  egin{equation} \la\Ylm|Y_\mp^\lpa_\Omega = \delta_{l\lp}\delta_{m\mp}, \end{equation}  egin{equation} \la\Ylm|Y_\mp^\lpa_\Omega = \delta_{l\lp}\delta_{m\mp}, \end{equation} where \la.|.a_\Omega \la.|.a_\Omega denotes the integration over the unit sphere. Furthermore, they form a complete basis set since any square-integrable function f(	heta,\phi) f(	heta,\phi) can be expanded in series according to:  egin{equation} f(	heta,\phi) = \sum_{l=0}^\infty \sum_{m=-l}^l a_{\lm}\Ylm(	heta,\phi). \end{equation}  egin{equation} f(	heta,\phi) = \sum_{l=0}^\infty \sum_{m=-l}^l a_{\lm}\Ylm(	heta,\phi). \end{equation} Complex spherical harmonics satisfy two useful properties commonly employed in practical applications. The parity properties:  egin{equation}\label{eq:parity_property_Ylm} {Y_m^l}^\dagger(rhat) = (-1)^m Y_{-m}^l (rhat), \end{equation}  egin{equation}\label{eq:parity_property_Ylm} {Y_m^l}^\dagger(rhat) = (-1)^m Y_{-m}^l (rhat), \end{equation}  egin{equation} \Ylm(-rhat) = (-1)^l \Ylm(rhat), \end{equation}  egin{equation} \Ylm(-rhat) = (-1)^l \Ylm(rhat), \end{equation} and the addition theorem:  egin{equation}\label{eq:addition_theorem_CSH} \sum_m \Ylm(rhat)\, \Ylm^\*(rphat) = \dfrac{2l+1}{4\pi}\,\mcP_l (rhat\cdotrphat), \end{equation}  egin{equation}\label{eq:addition_theorem_CSH} \sum_m \Ylm(rhat)\, \Ylm^\*(rphat) = \dfrac{2l+1}{4\pi}\,\mcP_l (rhat\cdotrphat), \end{equation} where \mcP_l(q) \mcP_l(q) are Legendre polynomials. Equation ef{eq:addition_theorem_CSH} is generally employed in the application of the nonlocal part of the Hamiltonian in the case of norm-conserving pseudopotentials when useylm = 0. Real spherical harmonics ¶ Real spherical harmonics (RSH) are obtained by combining complex conjugate functions associated to opposite values of m m . RSH are the most adequate basis functions for calculations in which atomic symmetry is important since they can be directly related to the irreducible representations of the subgroups of D_3 D_3 [Blanco1997] . Moreover, being real, they have half the memory requirement of complex spherical harmonics. This is clearly an advantage if high angular momenta are needed or several RHS values have to be stored in memory. A possible definition for RHS is [Blanco1997] :  egin{equation}\label{eq:Definition_real_harmonics} Ylm \df  egin{cases} \quad \dfrac{(-1)^m}{\sqrt{2}}\,(\Ylm + \Ylm^\*) \quad m > 0 \\ \quad Y_0^l \quad m = 0 \\ \quad \dfrac{(-1)^m}{i\sqrt{2}}\,(Y_{\lvert{m}vert}^l - {Y_{\lvert{m}vert}^l}^\*) \quad m < 0. \end{cases} \end{equation}  egin{equation}\label{eq:Definition_real_harmonics} Ylm \df  egin{cases} \quad \dfrac{(-1)^m}{\sqrt{2}}\,(\Ylm + \Ylm^\*) \quad m > 0 \\ \quad Y_0^l \quad m = 0 \\ \quad \dfrac{(-1)^m}{i\sqrt{2}}\,(Y_{\lvert{m}vert}^l - {Y_{\lvert{m}vert}^l}^\*) \quad m < 0. \end{cases} \end{equation} Equation ef{eq:Definition_real_harmonics} can be rewritten in matrix notation as  egin{equation}\label{eq:Unitaty_transformation_Y_Re2Cmplx} \underlineY^l = U^l \underline{Y}^l, \end{equation}  egin{equation}\label{eq:Unitaty_transformation_Y_Re2Cmplx} \underlineY^l = U^l \underline{Y}^l, \end{equation} where \underlineY^l \underlineY^l and \underline{Y}^l \underline{Y}^l are column vectors containing real and spherical harmonics ordered by increasing m m values. U^l U^l is a unitary matrix whose explicit expression can be found in [Blanco1997] . The basic properties of RSH can be easily derived from the properties of complex spherical harmonics by means of ef{eq:Definition_real_harmonics}. Reality :  egin{equation} {Ylm}^*(rhat) = Ylm(rhat), \end{equation} Parity property :  egin{equation} Ylm(-rhat) = (-1)^l Ylm (rhat), \end{equation} Orthonormality :  egin{equation} \laYlm|Y_{\mp}^\lpa_\Omega = \delta_{l\lp} \delta_{m\mp}. \end{equation} Certain on-site matrix elements include the integration of three real spherical harmonics, which can be evaluated exactly:  egin{equation}\label{eq:Gaunt_def} \Gaunt_{\li\mi\lj\mj}^{\LM} \df \int Y_\mi^\li\,Y_M^L\,Y_\mj^\lj\ddO. \end{equation}  egin{equation}\label{eq:Gaunt_def} \Gaunt_{\li\mi\lj\mj}^{\LM} \df \int Y_\mi^\li\,Y_M^L\,Y_\mj^\lj\ddO. \end{equation} The above integral is known in the literature under the name of Gaunt coefficient. Due to selection rules, the integral in equation ef{eq:Gaunt_def} is nonzero only if \mi+\mj = M \mi+\mj = M and \lvert{\li+\lj}vert \ge (L,L+2,L+4, \dots) \ge \lvert{\li-\lj}vert \lvert{\li+\lj}vert \ge (L,L+2,L+4, \dots) \ge \lvert{\li-\lj}vert . Note The expression for \mcG \mcG follows the internal conventions used inside the ABINIT code and slightly differs from the one reported in standard text-books. Symmetry transformation of RSH ¶ The use of symmetry properties is of fundamental importance to simplify the determination of electronic structure as it greatly reduces the number of non-equivalent integrals that have to be computed, as well as the size of any tables stored in memory. Frequently one has to evaluate the value of a RSH at a rotated point after the application of a symmetry operation belonging to the space group of the crystal. This usually occurs when \kk \kk -dependent matrix elements have to be symmetrized in the full Brillouin zone starting from the knowledge of their symmetrical image in the irreducible wedge. The effect of a proper or improper rotation on a RSH can be deduced from:  egin{equation} \label{eq:RSH_rotation} \omcR\,Ylm(rhat) = Ylm(\mcRm rhat) = \sum_\alpha D^l_{\alpha m}(\mcR)\,Ylm(rhat). \end{equation}  egin{equation} \label{eq:RSH_rotation} \omcR\,Ylm(rhat) = Ylm(\mcRm rhat) = \sum_\alpha D^l_{\alpha m}(\mcR)\,Ylm(rhat). \end{equation} That is, spherical harmonics of given l l are transformed into a linear combination of RHS of same l l where the coefficients are given by:  egin{equation} D^l_{\alpha m}(\mcR) \df \laY^l_\alpha|\hat\mcR|Ylma. \end{equation}  egin{equation} D^l_{\alpha m}(\mcR) \df \laY^l_\alpha|\hat\mcR|Ylma. \end{equation} Useful expansions in terms of RSH ¶ By means of the unitary transformation given in ef{eq:Definition_real_harmonics}, it is possible to rewrite the Rayleigh expansion of a plane wave in terms of RSH as  egin{equation} \label{eq:Rayleigh_expansion_real_Ylm} e^{i(\kpG)\cdotr} = 4\pi \sum_\lm i^l j_l(\lvert{\kpG}vert r)\,Ylm(\kpGhat)\,Ylm(rhat) \end{equation}  egin{equation} \label{eq:Rayleigh_expansion_real_Ylm} e^{i(\kpG)\cdotr} = 4\pi \sum_\lm i^l j_l(\lvert{\kpG}vert r)\,Ylm(\kpGhat)\,Ylm(rhat) \end{equation} with j_l(x) j_l(x) the spherical Bessel function of order l l . In a similar way, the electrostatic potentials can be expanded in a real spherical harmonics basis set. The final expression is very similar to the one obtained in the case of complex spherical harmonics:  egin{equation}\label{eq:Expansion_Coulombian_RSH} \dfrac{1}{\lvert{r-rp}vert} = \sum_{l=0}^\infty \dfrac{4\pi}{2l+1} \sum_{m=-l}^{m=l} \dfrac{r_<^l}{r_>^{l+1}} Ylm(rhat)Ylm(rphat), \end{equation}  egin{equation}\label{eq:Expansion_Coulombian_RSH} \dfrac{1}{\lvert{r-rp}vert} = \sum_{l=0}^\infty \dfrac{4\pi}{2l+1} \sum_{m=-l}^{m=l} \dfrac{r_<^l}{r_>^{l+1}} Ylm(rhat)Ylm(rphat), \end{equation} with r_< \df \min(r,r') r_< \df \min(r,r') and r_> \df \max(r,r') r_> \df \max(r,r') .

