equation H |\psi_{n{ f k}}a = \varepsilon_{n{ f k}}|\psi_{n{ f k}}a \,, H |\psi_{n{ f k}}a = \varepsilon_{n{ f k}}|\psi_{n{ f k}}a \,, where H H is the Hamiltonian operator, n n labels the state (or the band), \varepsilon_{n{ f k}} \varepsilon_{n{ f k}} is the eigenvalue. As the wavevector labelling of an eigenstate comes from the property \psi_{ f k}({ f r}+{ f R}) = e^{i{ f k}{ f R}} \psi_{ f k}({ f r}) \,, \psi_{ f k}({ f r}+{ f R}) = e^{i{ f k}{ f R}} \psi_{ f k}({ f r}) \,, in which \kk \kk can be replaced by \kk + \GG \kk + \GG where \GG \GG is any reciprocal space lattice vector, we can choose the wavefunctions at \kk \kk and \kk + \GG \kk + \GG to be equal, or to make a linear combination of wavefunctions with the same energy. This is a choice of gauge that does not affect the value of physical observables. In what follows we prefer to work with the gauge \psi_{\kk + \GG}(r) = \psi_\kk(r) \psi_{\kk + \GG}(r) = \psi_\kk(r) to keep the notation as simple as possible, The time-reversal symmetry (non-magnetic case) of the Hamiltonian gives the following relation: \psi_{n{ f k}}({ f r}) = \psi^{*}_{n- f k}({ f r}) \,. \psi_{n{ f k}}({ f r}) = \psi^{*}_{n- f k}({ f r}) \,. For the { f k} { f k} wavevectors that are half a reciprocal lattice vector (2{ f k}={ f G}_{0}) (2{ f k}={ f G}_{0}) , there is a special relationship between the Fourier coefficients of the wavefunction: u_{n{ f k}}({ f G}) = u_{n{ f k}-{ f G}_{0}}({ f G}+{ f G}_{0}) = u_{n-{ f k}}({ f G}+{ f G}_{0}) = u^{*}_{n{ f k}}(-{ f G}-{ f G}_{0}) \,. u_{n{ f k}}({ f G}) = u_{n{ f k}-{ f G}_{0}}({ f G}+{ f G}_{0}) = u_{n-{ f k}}({ f G}+{ f G}_{0}) = u^{*}_{n{ f k}}(-{ f G}-{ f G}_{0}) \,. That is, coefficients at \GG \GG and -{ f G}-{ f G}_{0} -{ f G}-{ f G}_{0} are related. This will allow to decrease by a factor of 2 the storage space for these specific { f k} { f k} points and accelerate CPU-intensive parts such as the application of the non-local part and the Fourier transform. Properties of